In article <Sep.5.00.58.27.1993.7264@math.rutgers.edu> muckenho@math.rutgers.edu (Ben Muckenhoupt) writes:

   A suggestion: Hypodermic syringes as items in NetHack.
   Applying an empty syringe would cause the game to prompt you for a
   potion from your inventory to fill it. Applying a full syringe
   would cause it to prompt you for a direction.

Nifty, and doesn't require any new commands.

   The primary use would be injecting monsters with paralysis,
   sickness, or whatever other nastiness springs to mind. This would be
   more effective than throwing the potion bottle at the monster, and the
   player would be guaranteed not to inhale the fumes.

But cursed syringes would inject the player some 20% of the time, normal
ones would break if used against armored/metallic monsters and blessed ones
would be Really Valuable[tm].

   Healers would have a certain chance of starting with syringes.
   Nurses would often carry them, as well.

A couple of ideas of my own:

Sunglasses, protection against yellow lights;
Mirrorshades, as sunglasses + 10% of reflecting a ray;
God-o-meter, shows (in colours) if you can safely pray;
Whetstone, removes rust (and unless you have the intrinsic Smithcraft,
	may damage the weapon.  Gnomish smiths in the town
	level will de-rust weapons for a price).
Wand of anything (counterpart to Wand of Nothing) - every charge
	creates a different effect (not wishing, however);
Ring of fasting - lowers food consumption to negligible, but
	severely abuses constitution;

A new class: Melnibonean,
	quest nemesis = Yyrkoon (with Mournblade) or Jagreen Lern
	pet = dragon (grows slowly),
	quest artifact = Chaos Shield of Mordaga (Immune to magic),
	alignment = chaotic
	high int, wis, cha; low str, con; carries several potions of
		strength;
	start out with a couple of spellbooks;
	the first weapon provided by god = Stormy;
	intrinsics = witch sight (sense power within an item),
		poison resistance,
		aggravate monster (??)
	Mini-quest = Slaying Theleb K'Aarna;

